A||N||5||Stan86SE||Interior Door Handle Plate Removal||||5||
Z||000000||Stan86SE||02-25-2001||08:55 PM||||<BR>Hello!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Man, am I ever glad that I stumbled across this Forum!  I just solved some problems I was having using the online service guide (Code 32), but finding this place and all of the experienced Fiero people here is an unexpected gold mine!<P>Now - I feel really stupid asking a question about something so simple - but, when taking off the interior door handle bezel...after removing the tabs and the two screws, what should I do next to get the plastic bezel off?  Does the cover of the sliding lock come off somehow, too?  I'm replacing the power lock switches for appearance, and I thought that it would be a 5-minute job.  I'm sure it _is_, but I didn't want to try to force anything off and end up breaking things, so I thought I'd better ask here first.  Anybody?  Thanks a lot!<P>- Stan  <p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 02-25-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||02-25-2001||09:37 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>No question is a dumb question here!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, you have a small round screw cover under the pull handle to pry up, and remove the screw underneath there.  The plastic Pegasus emblem is also a screw cover.  Now the sliding lock cover thingy...it kinda pulls off at the "bend" of it, and then pull it towards the front of the car.  You may have to pry it from behind with a small screwdriver.  As for replacing the power lock switches without breaking anything, good luck.  Be very careful, the things they snap into are supposed to be a 1 way deal, so once the factory puts them in there, someone hitting that switch real hard can't knock them out.  I've done it, you just have to be careful at what you pry at, and dont get into too much of a rush.<P>And just one last to watch out for, there is a difference between the left and right side switches, so make sure you're putting the switch into the correct side! :P<P>Hope this helps<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Stan86SE||02-25-2001||09:49 PM||||Hey, many thanks FieroJoe!  I really appreciate all the help and info!  I wasn't aware that the lock switch "sockets" were a one-way affair, so I'll try to be extra careful and take it slow and easy.  And, yeah, I've got new left and right switches (actually my old right one still looks pretty good), and with lotsa luck on a GOOD day, I can still sometimes finally manage to remember which side is which!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks again!<BR>Stan||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||02-25-2001||10:22 PM||||the sliding lock snaps onto the lock lever at the rear and the front of the sliding lock sits on a pin..........you can reach in behind the plate and pop the lever loose with a small flat blade screwdriver.......||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||02-25-2001||10:24 PM||||oh yeah, welcome to the Forum!!! I am originally from Lexington..........||||reg||1
Z||000005||Stan86SE||02-25-2001||10:49 PM||||Thanks for the welcome and the additional info, tgowens!  Your reply, along with FieroJoe's, gives me a good "visual" of how things are put together and how to go about the disassembly...exactly what I needed!  Nice to hear from a former Bluegrass State-r, too.  Lexington's a great place to be from!<P>- Stan||||reg||1
